dict(sec=35, img=None,
stem="A 74-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation who stopped her apixaban a month ago develops sudden severe pain in her right leg 3 hours ago. The leg is pale and cold, with no palpable femoral, popliteal or pedal pulses, decreased sensation of the foot, and weak toe movement.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate first step?",
choices=["Aspirin and repeat examination in 6 hours","Ankle–brachial index and outpatient referral","Heparin drip and emergency vascular surgery","Start cilostazol","Supervised exercise therapy"], key=2,
exp="Sudden <b>pain, pallor, pulselessness, paresthesia, paralysis and poikilothermia</b> (the six P's — \"probably the most important thing you're gonna have\") is <b>acute limb ischemia</b>, here from a cardiac <b>embolus</b> (atrial fibrillation off anticoagulation). <b>intravenous unfractionated heparin is always the first step</b>, with emergent vascular surgery: \"time = tissue.\" Sensory loss comes before motor loss; with weakness the limb is immediately threatened, so open (Fogarty balloon) embolectomy is preferred; with intact function there is time for catheter-directed thrombolysis or thrombectomy. Afterward, find the source and anticoagulate long-term.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Acute limb ischemia: intravenous heparin first and emergent vascular surgery; motor or sensory loss means the limb is immediately threatened.",
wrong={0:"Waiting allows irreversible muscle and nerve death.",
1:"This is an emergency, not an outpatient problem.",
3:"Cilostazol is for chronic claudication.",
4:"Exercise is for stable claudication, not an acutely threatened limb."},
eli5="A clot from the heart blocked the leg's main artery. Start heparin right away and get the surgeon to pull the clot out within hours."),

dict(sec=35, img='H_fasciotomy', cap="Intraoperative photograph of the lower leg",
stem="A 70-year-old man had a femoral embolectomy for acute limb ischemia that had lasted 7 hours. Four hours later his calf is tense and exquisitely painful, the pain worsens with passive stretching of the toes, and he has numbness between the first and second toes. Pedal pulses are palpable. The procedure performed next is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains his complication?",
choices=["Deep venous thrombosis","Re-embolization of the femoral artery","Reperfusion muscle swelling","Rhabdomyolysis-induced hyperkalemia","Wound infection"], key=2,
exp="After about <b>6–8 hours</b> of ischemia, <b>reperfusion</b> generates free radicals and capillary leak, and the muscle swells inside its unyielding fascia: <b>compartment syndrome</b>. Signs: <b>pain out of proportion and on passive stretch</b>, a tense compartment, and <b>numbness of the first web space</b> (deep peroneal nerve, anterior compartment). Pulses can remain palpable. Treatment is <b>fasciotomy</b> (shown: swollen muscle bulging through the released fascia). Rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalemia and kidney injury can follow.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Reperfusion after prolonged limb ischemia can cause compartment syndrome (pain on passive stretch, first web-space numbness); treat with fasciotomy.",
wrong={0:"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) causes swelling but not first web-space numbness with pain on passive stretch.",
1:"Re-embolization would abolish the pulses.",
3:"Hyperkalemia is a consequence, not the cause of the tense calf.",
4:"Infection develops over days with fever."},
eli5="When blood came back after hours of starvation, the leg muscles swelled inside their tight wrapping and squeezed themselves. Cutting the wrapping open relieves the pressure."),

